    "summary": "Mellow Shoreline show with strong Terrapin/Black Peter but uneven flow",
    "blurb": "The set opens sweetly with Iko Iko and includes a soaring Terrapin Station and a powerful Black Peter. The second half delivers solid Bertha, Playing In The Band and an engaging drums‑space segment, though the energy wanes toward the end. Overall the performance is mixed, offering a few bright moments amid generally flat pacing.",
    "review": "September 30, 1988 – Shoreline Amphitheatre: The Dead took the stage with a gentle opener, slipping into Iko Iko before wandering through bluesy territory on Walkin' Blues and Stagger Lee. Highlights emerged in the first set when Jerry’s guitar rose on Terrapin Station and his phrasing shone on Black Peter, while Brent's keyboards stayed in the background. The second set kicked off with a spirited Bertha and a seamless transition into Playing In The Band, leading to a well‑received drums‑space segment that added some spark. Yet the show never fully regained momentum; songs like Push Comes To Shove and Sugar Magnolia felt listless, and by the encore the energy had largely dissipated. Fans will find worthwhile moments—especially Terrapin Station, Black Peter, and the early Iko Iko—but should temper expectations for a consistently high‑energy experience.\n\nFor listeners seeking the clearest capture, the soundboard recording (gd88-09-30.set2-sbd.sacks.7585.sbeok.shnf) offers solid clarity and showcases the strongest parts of the performance without the muffled mix that plagues other sources.",
